okay today I'm going to talk about the  topic in p1 about ultrasound and  infrasound now first of all we need to  know a little bit about sound waves I've  drawn two different sound waves here  so just to recap sound waves are  longitudinal longitudinal waves this  means that the direction of the  vibrations is the same direction that  the wave is traveling so they're in the  same direction now what we can see here  is we can actually see there's different  compressions in either wave so on this  top wave we can see we can measure the  wavelength which is the distance between  these different compressions so the  wavelength of the top one is quite short  whereas the wavelength for the bottom  one is quite long now this shorter the  wavelength the higher the frequency now  what ultrasound actually is is sound  that cannot be heard the frequency is  too high to be detected by the human ear  so it's any frequency above 20,000 Hertz  Hertz is just the units for frequency so  infrasound is like the opposite its  wavelength is very long meaning it has a  very low frequency it's anything which  is below 20 Hertz so again we can't hear  this sound because it's too low  frequency so we can actually use  infrasound and ultrasound for different  things you could use ultrasound for baby  scans and also for sonar in submarines  that we're going to look at in a minute  an infrasound is used by elephants to  communicate with other members of their  herd because basically this one could  travel over travel over long distances  and be diffracted around objects easily  so this one could be used by elephants  also meteor strikes and volcanic  eruptions produce infrasound which can  be used to detect them  really far away so that's the basics of  infrasound and ultrasound so we're going  to quickly now have a look how  ultrasound can be used in submarines as  sonar so what we have right here is  you'll see my beautiful joins underneath  the sea we can have a submarine  now this submarine can use sonar to try  and detect how far away different  objects are so this is how it works it  releases it emits our ultrasound which  travels down to the bottom of the seabed  the seafloor due to the high frequency a  lot of this sound is reflected back and  can then be detected by the submarine so  this here with my beautiful submarine  now we could actually use an equation  that we should have seen before to find  out exactly how far away this the sea  floor is by doing this equation speed  equals distance over time now we know or  rather we will be given this in the exam  we did not need to know to remember this  the speed of sound in sea water is 1520  meters per second so that's just how  fast this ultrasound is traveling  through the water so let's say let's say  in this particular instance it took ten  seconds for the ultrasound to hit the  hip the seafloor and reflect back up and  be detected by the submarine so this is  what we need to do because we're looking  to try and find the distance to the  seafloor  we cover up our distance so we now have  speed times time  so we're going to do 1520 multiplied by  10 which gives us  fifteen thousand two hundred meters now  this isn't our final answer because  what's that's told us is the distance  all the way from the submarine to the  seafloor and then all the way from the  seafloor back up to the submarine so all  that we need to do now is we just need  to divide that answer by two to find the  distance just to the seafloor so we're  doing fifteen thousand two hundred  meters because that was the distance  from here back up again divided by two  and the answer is seven thousand six  hundred meters so that is one very  important use of ultrasound thank you
 

